1
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:26,252
MATTHEW: Toledo, Ohio. The Glass City. Home of the Rockets. And for a couple of seasons in the middle of the two thousands, home of the cheapest fix in college sports.

2
00:00:26,552 --> 00:00:35,926
GARY MERCER: Point shaving? That is a big-city racket. Boston College. The mob. Not Toledo. And I am a Bowling Green man, I would love to believe it.

3
00:00:41,404 --> 00:00:44,493
TESSA MORALES: Gary, it is an hour up the road from Detroit.

4
00:00:44,793 --> 00:00:54,289
MATTHEW: Two gamblers from the Detroit area. Ghazi Manni and his partner, Mitchell Karam. From about 2004 to 2006 they made friends with Toledo football and basketball players.

5
00:00:54,589 --> 00:01:00,152
TESSA MORALES: And they did not need much to do it. Players told the court they took money, meals and groceries.

6
00:01:00,452 --> 00:01:01,535
GARY MERCER: Groceries.

7
00:01:01,835 --> 00:01:09,243
TESSA MORALES: Groceries, Gary. These were college kids without a dime. Somebody buys you dinner and asks how the team looks this week.

8
00:01:09,543 --> 00:01:20,518
MATTHEW: Then he asks for a little more. And nobody had to lose a game. That is the part people get wrong about point shaving. You win by six instead of ten. The scoreboard says Toledo, and the gambler cashes anyway.

9
00:01:20,818 --> 00:01:29,693
TESSA MORALES: One running back took more than two thousand dollars in cash and goods. By the government's account, that included one hundred dollars to fumble in the 2005 GMAC Bowl.

10
00:01:29,993 --> 00:01:33,184
GARY MERCER: A hundred dollars? In a bowl game? On television?

11
00:01:33,484 --> 00:01:39,656
MATTHEW: One hundred dollars. And here is the part I can't get past. Toledo won that game forty-five to thirteen.

12
00:01:39,956 --> 00:01:43,403
GARY MERCER: They won by thirty-two and a fella still got paid to drop the ball.

13
00:01:43,703 --> 00:01:48,608
TESSA MORALES: That is how small it is, Gary. One play. Nobody in the stadium would ever know.

14
00:01:48,908 --> 00:01:53,199
TESSA MORALES: The government counted about four hundred thousand dollars in bets on Toledo basketball alone.

15
00:01:53,499 --> 00:01:56,906
GARY MERCER: So the players got lunch money and the gamblers got rich.

16
00:01:57,206 --> 00:02:10,004
TESSA MORALES: And here is who caught it, Gary. A Las Vegas casino. In October 2005, MGM saw strange money on Toledo games and called the Nevada regulators. By that December the FBI was listening to the gambler's phone.

17
00:02:10,304 --> 00:02:12,367
GARY MERCER: The casino called the cops.

18
00:02:12,667 --> 00:02:21,175
MATTHEW: That is the usual arrangement. Seven former Rockets pleaded guilty. The judge gave them probation. He said what they did was out of character, and it was ten years gone.

19
00:02:21,475 --> 00:02:29,445
MATTHEW: One of them stood outside the courthouse and said this. I had issues, gambling issues. I was depressed at the time. I made poor decisions.

20
00:02:29,745 --> 00:02:33,226
GARY MERCER: Well. That is a kid talking. That is not a gangster.

21
00:02:33,526 --> 00:02:40,579
MATTHEW: And one more thing belongs in the record. In 2006 a player was asked to help fix a game against Iowa State. He said no.

22
00:02:40,879 --> 00:02:42,732
TESSA MORALES: Nobody wrote a headline about him.

23
00:02:43,032 --> 00:02:50,051
MATTHEW: The gambler Manni got seventy months in federal prison, on a plea that also covered fixed horse races and food stamp fraud.

24
00:02:50,351 --> 00:02:56,093
TESSA MORALES: Now think about when this happened. Sports betting was against the law almost everywhere. You had to know a guy.

25
00:03:01,339 --> 00:03:13,663
MATTHEW: Today the guy is an app. DraftKings and FanDuel are on the broadcast, on the scoreboard, and on the phone in your pocket. You can bet a Tuesday night MAC game, right down to one player's rebounds.

26
00:03:13,963 --> 00:03:18,754
GARY MERCER: Well, at least it is all legal and out in the open now. That ought to keep it clean.

27
00:03:19,054 --> 00:03:20,076
TESSA MORALES: Does it, Gary?

28
00:03:20,377 --> 00:03:35,192
MATTHEW: Right now the Cleveland Guardians' closer is on trial in Brooklyn, charged with selling his pitches for a few thousand dollars. Terry Rozier, an NBA guard, goes to trial in February. Both men say they are not guilty. And notice whose names you know. The player. It is always the player.

29
00:03:35,492 --> 00:03:42,579
TESSA MORALES: That closer had a twenty million dollar contract. Prosecutors say he also owed the IRS half a million dollars.

30
00:03:42,879 --> 00:03:58,017
MATTHEW: Now look up the food chain. Last year Massachusetts regulators found that the legal sportsbooks cut down the bets of customers who win too often. The sharp ones. The ones who do the homework. And they hand the VIP status and the rewards mostly to the people who lose.

31
00:03:58,317 --> 00:04:02,055
GARY MERCER: Hold on. They throw out the winners and give the losers a host?

32
00:04:02,355 --> 00:04:09,759
TESSA MORALES: One man lost one point eight million dollars, Gary. His FanDuel VIP manager sent him a personal video from a big league star.

33
00:04:10,059 --> 00:04:19,081
MATTHEW: And how did all of this get legal? The New York Times found the industry's lobbyists lavished state lawmakers with gifts, parties and millions of dollars in donations.

34
00:04:19,381 --> 00:04:33,113
MATTHEW: So a college kid takes a hundred dollars, and it is a federal case. A company limits the winners, rewards the losers, and wines and dines the statehouse, and it is a business model. Ask yourself again. Who is the real criminal?

35
00:04:33,413 --> 00:04:36,457
GARY MERCER: A hundred dollars to fumble. I take it back about Toledo.

36
00:04:36,757 --> 00:04:40,606
MATTHEW: We run betting lines on this site. So we ought to be the first ones to say it out loud.

37
00:04:40,906 --> 00:04:43,375
MATTHEW: Toledo, Ohio. Keep it real.
